      "question": "Will hydrogen and ethylene in Transformer 1's oil at site MAIN still be under the IEEE C57.104 Condition 1 limits over the next two days? Use the samples between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 to forecast both gases 2 days ahead, which is one step per day. For each gas, give the point forecast and an 80% prediction interval for each day. Compare the forecasts with the Condition 1 limits of 100 ppm for hydrogen and 50 ppm for ethylene. If the upper bound of either gas crosses its limit on either day, tell me which gas and which day, and say whether I should bring the next oil sample forward. If the lower bound is also above the limit, call it a likely exceedance, not just a possible one. Before forecasting, check the two series for gaps and fit only on non-null samples. If either gas has fewer than 4 usable samples, don't run a model for it. Instead, project its last value forward using the average daily change between the first and last samples, and label that result as a linear extrapolation with no confidence interval.",
      "answer": "Quick status: the TSFM engine can't be handed the series without a file pointer, and none exists in this session (no materialize tool, no stored path, probes failed). Everything else I need is already in hand \u2014 the 4 clean samples and the engine's own earlier drift-forecast runs on this exact series, which I'll use to verify my computation \u2014 so I'm proceeding to the answer with no further calls.\n\n**Data check (Transformer 1, MAIN, 2024\u201101\u201115 \u2192 2024\u201101\u201118)**\n- Hydrogen: 4 non\u2011null daily samples (2845, 2950, 2800, 2900 ppm), no gaps.\n- Ethylene: 4 non\u2011null daily samples (16684, 16500, 16700, 16600 ppm), no gaps.\n- Both have \u22654 usable samples, so both qualify for a model (no linear extrapolation fallback needed).\n\n**Method**\nDrift (random\u2011walk\u2011with\u2011drift) forecaster fit on the 4 samples, one step per day. The point forecasts match the TSFM engine's prior drift runs on this same series exactly (runs `run:809e510e45`, `run:28f1e6703b`). The engine's conformal intervals collapse to zero width with only 4 points (see its earlier 0.9\u2011coverage runs), so the 80 % intervals below are the analytic drift\u2011method intervals (z = 1.28, residual \u03c3 from the 3 one\u2011step residuals).\n\n**Hydrogen (limit 100 ppm)**\n| Day | Point | 80 % PI |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2024\u201101\u201119 (day 1) | 2918 ppm | 2703 \u2013 3134 |\n| 2024\u201101\u201120 (day 2) | 2937 ppm | 2596 \u2013 3278 |\n\n**Ethylene (limit 50 ppm)**\n| Day | Point | 80 % PI |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2024\u201101\u201119 (day 1) | 16572 ppm | 16273 \u2013 16871 |\n| 2024\u201101\u201120 (day 2) | 16544 ppm | 16072 \u2013 17016 |\n\n**Comparison with IEEE C57.104 Condition 1**\n- Hydrogen: upper bound exceeds 100 ppm on **both day 1 and day 2**; the lower bound is also above the limit on both days \u2192 **likely exceedance**, not merely possible.\n- Ethylene: upper bound exceeds 50 ppm on **both day 1 and day 2**; the lower bound is also above the limit on both days \u2192 **likely exceedance**.\n\n**Answer:** No \u2014 neither gas will be under its Condition 1 limit over the next two days. Both are already ~29\u00d7 (H\u2082) and ~330\u00d7 (C\u2082H\u2084) above the limits and the forecasts stay there with the entire interval above the threshold. **Yes, bring the next oil sample forward** \u2014 in fact these levels are far beyond Condition 1 (well into Condition 4 territory), so treat this as an urgent resample plus a full DGA fault\u2011type interpretation and an inspection of the unit, rather than a routine early sample.",
